ie.manual.category.immersivenuclear.name=Immersive Nuclear
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_processor.name=Chemical Processing Unit
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_processor.subtext=Acid attack
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_processor0=The Chemical Processing Unit attacks uranium powder with reagent and water and turns it into slurry. The structure is built as shown above and formed by using the Engineer's Hammer on the redstone block at the front.
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_processor1=Each batch takes 16 uranium powder, 16 processing reagent and 8000 mB of water and gives 8000 mB of uranium slurry, which goes on to the <link;in_filter_press;Filtration Unit>. Water goes in on the right; powder and reagent at the back; the slurry comes out on the left. Energy goes into the heavy engineering blocks.<br>A redstone signal on the control panel halts the machine; the hammer inverts this behaviour.
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_processor2=The water port also takes chemical or depleted effluent, or the Industrial Dryer's process condensate, stored in its own recycled liquor tank. Up to 4000 mB of each batch can come from it instead of water. With effluent the batch uses less reagent (up to 4 less); the condensate only replaces water. It is the simplest place to send the <link;in_byproducts;effluent>.
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_processor3=Processing reagent: sulfur and saltpeter, both from IE's Crusher.
ie.manual.entry.in_filter_press.name=Filtration Unit
ie.manual.entry.in_filter_press.subtext=Under pressure
ie.manual.entry.in_filter_press0=The Filtration Unit is a filter press: it holds back the solids of the slurry and lets the solution through. The structure is built as shown above and formed by using the Engineer's Hammer on the redstone block at the front.
ie.manual.entry.in_filter_press1=Each batch: 1000 mB of slurry gives 800 mB of crude uranium solution and 1 wet mineral residue. The plates close, the pump pressurizes and the solution passes through the filters; when the plates open, the residue drops onto the conveyor and leaves at the side. Slurry goes in at the front on the pump end; the solution comes out at the other end.
ie.manual.entry.in_filter_press2=The machine needs 28 filters, one per plate. Every batch wears each filter by 1; a spent filter stops filtering. At the back are an input for new filters and an output for spent ones, to automate the swap.<br>It also filters neutralized sludge: 1000 mB gives 1 neutralization cake and 800 mB of plain water, one kind of feed at a time.
ie.manual.entry.in_filter_press3=The filter: hemp fabric, treated wood and steel rods.
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_separator.name=Chemical Separation Unit
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_separator.subtext=Oil and water don't mix
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_separator0=The Chemical Separation Unit takes the uranium out of the crude solution with a solvent, in two mixer-settlers. The structure is built as shown above and formed by using the Engineer's Hammer on the redstone engineering block at the front.
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_separator1=In the first stage the uranium moves from the crude solution into the extraction solvent; in the second, the stripping solution takes it back out of the solvent. Out come purified uranium solution and chemical effluent. The solvent runs in a closed loop: only the small loss of each batch has to be topped up.
ie.manual.entry.in_chemical_separator2=Crude solution and stripping solution go in on the left end and the solvent at the back; purified solution and effluent come out on the right end. Energy goes into the heavy engineering blocks.<br>Solvent and stripping solution come from IE's Mixer; see <link;in_supplies;Supplies>.
ie.manual.entry.in_metal_extractor.name=Metal Extractor
ie.manual.entry.in_metal_extractor.subtext=Nothing goes to waste
ie.manual.entry.in_metal_extractor0=The Metal Extractor recovers the metals dissolved in the chemical effluent by electrolysis. The structure is built as shown above and formed by using the Engineer's Hammer on the redstone engineering block at the front.
ie.manual.entry.in_metal_extractor1=Each batch: the vat fills with 12000 mB of effluent, the current settles and the metals plate out on the six plates for a minute. While the depleted effluent drains, the plates rise and the scraper cleans them: 6 mixed metal deposits per batch. Effluent goes in on the left end, depleted effluent out on the right, the deposit out at the back.
ie.manual.entry.in_metal_extractor2=The machine only runs with six electrode plates in their slots; they can be mixed. Steel is the base; lead-silver gives more deposit with less energy and lasts twice as long; electrum-coated gives the most and lasts the longest. Every batch wears each plate, and a worn-through plate breaks.<br>Crush the deposit in IE's Crusher to get the metals back.
ie.manual.entry.in_metal_extractor3=Each better plate is made from the one before.
ie.manual.entry.in_supplies.name=Supplies
ie.manual.entry.in_supplies.subtext=It all comes from IE
ie.manual.entry.in_supplies0=Processing reagent: sulfur and saltpeter, from IE's Crusher.<br><br>Quicklime: diorite burnt in IE's Coke Oven. If a mod adds limestone, it burns into quicklime too, giving twice as much.
ie.manual.entry.in_supplies1=Extraction solvent: biodiesel, from IE's Refinery, with saltpeter in IE's Mixer.<br><br>Stripping solution: water and saltpeter in IE's Mixer.<br><br>Precipitating agent: quicklime and saltpeter, for the <link;in_precipitation_unit;Precipitation Unit>.
ie.manual.entry.in_supplies2=The precipitating agent.
ie.manual.entry.in_byproducts.name=Residues and by-products
ie.manual.entry.in_byproducts.subtext=Nothing is thrown away
ie.manual.entry.in_byproducts0=The chemical effluent of the separation cannot be dumped: if its tank fills up, the separation stops. It has three places to go: back to the <link;in_chemical_processor;Chemical Processing Unit> as recycled liquor, through the <link;in_metal_extractor;Metal Extractor>, or neutralized with quicklime in IE's Mixer into neutralized sludge.
ie.manual.entry.in_byproducts1=Neutralized sludge goes through the <link;in_filter_press;Filtration Unit> and becomes neutralization cake and plain water. In IE's Mixer the cake takes the clay's place in concrete.<br>The depleted effluent leaving the Extractor is still acidic: send it back to the chemical processing or neutralize it the same way.
ie.manual.entry.in_byproducts2=The wet mineral residue from the filtration is still soaked in acid: neutralize 4 residue with 1 quicklime. Neutralized residue goes into IE's Crusher (sand and a chance of iron, clay and a little uranium) or into a block: 4 neutralized residue, or 4 cakes, make 1 impure agglomerate.
ie.manual.entry.in_byproducts3=Neutralizing the residue, and the impure agglomerate.
ie.manual.entry.in_hazards.name=Hazard warnings
ie.manual.entry.in_hazards.subtext=Read the label
ie.manual.entry.in_hazards0=The materials of the line show hazard warnings in the item or bucket description. For now they only inform.<br><br>Radioactive (weak): slurry, crude and purified solutions, wet residue, concentrate and yellowcake.<br><br>Traces of radioactivity: effluent, sludge, cake and agglomerate; depleted uranium and its hexafluoride.
ie.manual.entry.in_hazards1=Corrosive: slurry, crude and purified solutions, effluent, depleted effluent, sulfuric acid and hydrofluoric acid.<br><br>Caustic: quicklime.<br><br>Oxidizer: processing reagent, sulfuric acid and oxygen.<br><br>Hot: steam.<br><br>Flammable: extraction solvent and hydrogen.<br><br>Toxic if inhaled: concentrate, yellowcake, fluorination ash and depleted uranium oxide; hydrofluoric acid and the uranium hexafluorides (toxic and corrosive on contact too).
