ie.manual.entry.in_electrolyzer.name=Electrolyzer
ie.manual.entry.in_electrolyzer.subtext=Hydrogen and oxygen from water
ie.manual.entry.in_electrolyzer0=The Electrolyzer splits water into hydrogen, for the Conversion Unit, and oxygen. The structure is built as shown above; hit the redstone engineering block (front side) with the Engineer's Hammer to form it.
ie.manual.entry.in_electrolyzer1=The rectifier turns the power into the strong direct current of the two cell stacks, where the water splits in a lye electrolyte. The gases rise with the electrolyte into their separators; the electrolyte goes back to the cells and the electrolyte tank keeps them full. Each 5 s cycle takes 250 mB of water and gives 500 mB of hydrogen and 250 mB of oxygen, drawing 160 IF/t; steam takes only 110 IF/t, as part of the energy is already in it.
ie.manual.entry.in_electrolyzer2=Water or steam goes in on the north side; the oxygen comes out at the back's north corner and the hydrogen at its south corner; energy goes into the heavy engineering blocks at the rectifier's end. A full hydrogen tank stops the machine, while a full oxygen tank lifts its relief valve and the oxygen is vented. A redstone signal stops it (the hammer inverts that).
jei.immersivenuclear.electrolyzer.info=5x7x5 multiblock (106 blocks, of them 4 Chemical Engineering Blocks). Build the structure and hit the redstone engineering block (front side) with the Engineer's Hammer. Water (or steam, which takes less power) in on the north side; oxygen and hydrogen out at the back (oxygen at the north corner, hydrogen at the south corner); energy on the heavy engineering blocks at the rectifier's end. Each cycle (provisional, 5 s): 250 mB of water give 500 mB of hydrogen and 250 mB of oxygen. When the oxygen tank is full its relief valve vents it; when the hydrogen tank is full the machine stops. A redstone signal stops the machine (the hammer inverts that).

